  "published": "2026-11-11T22:41:55.293Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v1.4.1: Validating a CNPJ is about twice as fast for numeric CNPJs and 40% faster for alphanumeric ones, as measured on .NET 10 and 11. A CNPJ that holds a letter or digit outside ASCII is now invalid: an accented letter, the Turkish İ or ı, a fullwidth digit, and also a superscript digit or a combining accent. IsValid used to skip them like punctuation, while the constructor kept the letters and digits in the value, and turned the long s (ſ) into an S, which made the value an invalid CNPJ. Cnpj.Create, which already rejected accented letters, now also rejects the long s, combining accents and superscript digits. A Cnpj read from XML is now stored as the constructor stores it: written in lowercase, or with punctuation in place of a leading zero, it did not equal the same CNPJ parsed from a string. Punctuation and spaces, ASCII or not, are still ignored, and Cpf is unchanged.",
